  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nNot a lot of change in the forecast evolution for Sunday with\nthe latest model suite. It appears that flash flooding would get\nthe nod over severe weather if you had to put the threats in \norder. \n\nFlash flooding:\nA plume of high PWATS (> greater two inches) is forecast to\noverspread most of the region Sunday. Meanwhile, the vertically\nstacked cyclone allows southerly wind flow to become parallel to\nthe baroclinic setting the stage for training. HREF 95th precip\nputs pockets of over 4\" across parts of northwest PA, while LPMM\nhas several pockets of 3\". We can't take the locations lock\nstep, but this does highlight the potential to see between a 10\nto 20 year rainfall return interval. Probability of 3\" or more\nof rain from the HREF with a 25 miles neighborhood radius\nresides between 10% to 15%. All this said, we may need to hoist\na short fuse flood watch as this event unfolds. The area of most\nconcern would be northwestern PA given paintball plots of\nhighest QPF. \n\nThere will be upper level support through ageostrophic wind \ndivergence at H2 thanks to favorable jet location. It will be \nan efficient rain process with warm cloud depths over 11-12KFT. \nInteresting enough, the probability of 1\" per hour rain rates \nare low, so it shows more of repeated rounds of storms will get \nplaces into potential trouble for streams coming out of their \nbanks and / or road closures. With the dry run recently, flash \nflood guidance for three hours is running around 2.00\" while the\nurban guidance of 0.75\" in one hour remains the same. \n\nSevere thunderstorms:\nThe trends continues to be a less favorable shear profile for\npotential weak tornadoes given hodographs and SRH values. Friday\nit was because of a back veer back setup and today more speed\nshear challenges. Nonetheless, a damaging wind threat does exist\ngiven 30-35 knots of deep-layer shear and MLCAPES around 1.0\nkjkg-1. The amount of storms depends on how much diurnal heating\nwe observe tomorrow. Looking upstream over parts of IND ad KY\nwere the dry slot is allowing for breaks in the clouds,\nextrapolation brings that across eastern Ohio and possibly into\nnorthern WV and western PA Sunday afternoon. This would create\nsufficient heating to fire off storms that could knock down\ntrees and wires. The way the CAMs are playing things, I could\nsee a scenario where the severe thunderstorm potential resides\nover our Ohio counties and the excessive rain threat stays in\nnorthwest PA. Either way, there will be a break between showers\novernight and the storms Sunday afternoon. The longer the break\nand more sunshine we see, the higher chance of storms some of\nwhich could be severe. \n\nMachine learning is mostly in line with SPC with nadocast\nputting a 15% chance of severe wind over western PA and the \nnorthern WV Panhandle. Depending on the how much we clear out and\nhow long goes a long way to putting the pieces of this atmospheric\npuzzle together. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe slow moving upper level low finally kicks out of here mid \nweek, however broad troughing over the Great Lakes quickly\nestablishes. This spells a forecast with a continued chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms every day next week. The time window \nwhen they are most likely to happen is during peak heating \nhours. As stated in the key messages above, no all day rains are\nforecast and some locations may not seen a drop all week. There\nwill be periods of sunshine as well, so don't put away the \nsunglasses just because you see a chance of rain in the \nforecast. \n\nTemperatures will be above average all week as humidity returns.\nDaytime highs will be widespread in the 80s with some\ncommunities hitting 90F in West Virginia and eastern Ohio.\nOvernight lows won't get below 65 unless you reside north of I80\nor in the mountains of southwestern PA and northern WV. As of\nnow, no organized severe weather is expected.",
